  {
    "episode_id": 97115,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97115.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Well, it started as a project to celebrate turning 60 and 25 years in public life. So I turned 60 in 2018 in the middle of a re-election campaign. So I couldn't really celebrate that year. But I said, when I'm done with this campaign, win or lose, I'm going to do something special to celebrate turning 60 and 25 years since my first city council race. The idea just popped into my head, Michael, after that race was over. Why not? create a nature quest that's Virginia based. My sister and brother-in-law in New York and their kids had done something called the 46ers, which is there's 46 peaks in the Adirondacks higher than 4,000 feet. And some New Yorkers just make it a quest to do all of them. And John and Taylor did it with their kids and dogs. There's a similar quest in Colorado to climb the 14ers, all the peaks that are higher than 14,000 feet. So I thought, I want to create one of these quests for Virginia. What would it be? And then I thought, well, okay. Walk ride paddle, walk on the 560 miles of the Appalachian Trail from Harpers Ferry to Tennessee, cycle from the Virginia North Carolina border on the Blue Ridge parkway in Skyline Drive all the way to Front Royal, and then canoe the whole James River. So this is my love letter to Virginia. My home of 40 years is to create this quest and do it. And I kept a journal thinking I might write about it, thinking that maybe somebody would be interested. But I think what kind of turned it into something more than just an account of each day's journey is between when I started, May of 2019 and when I finished, I was a juror into impeachment trials, attack on the capital, COVID and long COVID, the murder of George Floyd, contested presidential election. The nature journey became the kind of window through which I was looking at this unusual chapter in American life. And it was also what I was not just thinking about, but talking with people about along the way. So it's a weird mixture of a nature journey and a political reflection and Virginia history and conversations with companions and pastors by. So why did I write it? To challenge myself at a milestone in birthday and a milestone in my public life and to get to know my Commonwealth even better.",
