    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97115.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "say, I love that you thought about Zen in the Art of Motorcycle. I mean, that's a book that I absolutely love and I hadn't thought about for probably 20 years. And so that was not in my mind writing this book. There are lots of obvious parallels between that book and your book. When you say it, it's like, yeah, I guess I can see it because you're right. It's a journey, but it's much more than the journey. I would say the biggest influence on my thinking about this was a week on the Concord and Merrimack Rivers by Thoreau. That's one of my favorite books. And it's an amazing story. Thoreau and his brother just put in a boat. I think they built the boat and they filled it up with watermelons and food from their garden. They put in the boat in Concord and they canoeed down the Concord River to the confluence with the Merrimack. And then they take the boat upstream on the Merrimack into New Hampshire. And it's just a week-long journey. Thoreau kept notes about the journey, but didn't do anything with it for many years. His brother died. But this is what Thoreau worked on when he was at Walden. So we know Thoreau Walden is his most known work. But he didn't write Walden until after his sojourn there. What he did at Walden was take the notes of this river journey and he wrote it up. And each day is the journey and his reflections on his brother. It's an allergy to his brother who's passed on the nature of friendship, issues going on in the country at the time. I would say that book was the one thing I really thought about. And then the other thing to some degree is Teddy Roosevelt. Teddy Roosevelt wrote a lot of nature literature when he was in public office. He wrote about his time with the Rough Riders. He wrote about his time in the Dakotas. He wrote about hunting trips and things that he would take. When Teddy Roosevelt found out that he was going to be president, he was camping in the Adirondacks. McKinley had been shot Teddy had gone to Buffalo to see the president, thought the president was recovering, then went camping in the mountains of the Adirondack Mountains. And the Secret Service had to run up a trail and inform him that he was now president of the United States. He wrote a lot about nature while he was in public office. And I guess I was thinking a lot of my colleagues write books that, like, I'm introducing myself to the American public because I might be your president one day. The political books kind of fit into a a fairly narrow, straight jacket these days. I'm a politician, but this isn't that kind of book. It is a nature book. And I would say the throw and then the Teddy Roosevelt books were the things that were probably on my mind as I was writing it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
