    "turn_text": "I'm showing them on a bookshelf, the three old guides, somebody with the Potomac Appalachian Trail Club, which is one of the clubs, there's about 30 of these clubs that maintain stretches of the 2,200 mile Appalachian Trail. And Patsy is one of the most kind of comprehensive in the mileage that they maintain. Somebody heard about my trip, gave me the Potomac Appalachian Trail Club's Guide to the Virginia portion that was published in 1959. So I was born in 1958. So it's kind of like this is the 60th birthday thing. So this is what they were saying about the trail. when I was born. And I loved that with me, the first two-thirds of the Appalachian Trail hike, until we got to a place where the trail had been significantly rerouted, and it was no longer the version that had been done back in the 50s. But it was fascinating to read the old guide, both their advice for camping, like, don't wear long pants. I mean, don't wear shorts. They said, don't wear shorts. Are you kidding me? I'm hiking in August. What do you mean, don't wear shorts? There were, you know, archaic racial references at the time, to segregated facilities, or the refusal to acknowledge, like the campground along the A.T. in the Shenandoah National Park that was for African Americans, that the trail runs right through it. It's an amazing campground with a lot of amenities now and then and it doesn't even really get a mention while you cross a road crossing and it says, you know, Mrs. Nellie Linden has a house a half a mile away and we'll be glad to entertain a camper that they would tell you about the person down the road who would help you but not mention that the trail's going right next to a campground for African Americans showed that they assumed who would be using the guide, who would be using the trail, and it was still very much a piece of its time. So in that guide, my old guide who came along with me, some things about it were exactly the same when I did it in 2019, 60 years later, and some things about it were completely different.",
