    "turn_text": "do that. Or even just noting, hey, you shouldn't be looking at your phone. This is a big deal. But what it meant was I wasn't getting the warnings that all my other colleagues were getting. Their staffs were texting them, hey, there's an attack on the Capitol. This is getting ugly. I'm sitting there with a smile on my face, you know, blithely listening to the testimony, knowing that shortly we're going to confirm Joe Biden and Kamala Harris as President and Vice President. What happens then is Mitt Romney gets up and bolts out of the room. and then it immediately turns around and comes back in. Mike Pence is rushed out of the chair, but Chuck Grassley comes in as the president pro tem to preside. I assumed that Pence was getting one last phone call from Donald Trump trying to arm twist him. But then the sergeant arms takes the stand and says, we have to go and recess the capitals under attack. And that begins a very long 10 hours, maybe 12 hours where we are first barricaded in the chamber for about 45 minutes to an hour before they can clear a path for us to go over to one of the Senate office buildings to go into a committee room. This is the peak of COVID. 100 senators, 50 Senate staff jammed into this room, everybody wearing a mask except Senator Rand Paul were all jammed in there together. They roll in TV monitors and turn on and now for the first time I can see what this attack looks like. And I'm immediate like, I gotta call in and call my parents and tell them I'm okay. And I borrowed Martin Heinrich's phone to start making calls. So yeah, I don't leave home without it, I guess is the motto.",
