  {
    "episode_id": 97115,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97115.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "They definitely changed. So I started this hike in 2019, and I have lived for many years in Richmond and for 30 years in a house in Ginter Park neighborhood, Confederate Avenue. It's Confederate Avenue because the street is where the Confederacy had their intermediate line of defenses protecting the city of Richmond. It wasn't named after a person, but there's still earthwork burns in the neighborhood when the neighborhood was built. And when I moved onto the street and I guess it was, let's see, 1992, you know, I didn't think that much about it. I was on the Richmond City Council and Mayor and we knew we needed to do some things to kind of reckon with our history. And I viewed it as, I think I write about it this way, I viewed it as an addition problem, the key wasn't changing things or taking things down. But it was adding names, adding statues, when we built a new court building, naming an after a civil rights hero, and we built new bridges, naming it after people who, people of color, people who weren't Confederates. But I kind of got convinced by some staffers of mine in 2020 after the murder of George Floyd, the effort really picked up to tackle the issue of the monuments in Richmond. And one of my staffers said something to me, he goes, look, you look at the, you don't want to whitewash history, I get that. And you look at the monuments on Monument Avenue, as kind of a part of our painful past that we shouldn't whitewash, we should just be honest about it. He said, but for me, my worry is that it's not just part of the past. If we maintain them, we cut the lawn around them, we keep them beautiful, we have light display on them, we mark it and deter us. For me, it's not a symbol of a painful past. It's maybe that in the present, we still venerate people who would hold others in slavery. And then that makes me worry about what the future is. And so one of my staffers helped me see it differently. My wife and I then got involved in the effort in our neighborhood, Confederate Avenue is about four blocks long to change the name of the street. And it was interesting, Michael, here's a lesson. It was pretty easy at that point to get consensus. You're right. We should change the name of the street. But the harder thing is you got to have a new name. And so it was very hard to come up with a new name. I think there might have been 65 houses on our street. People submitted names. There were like 32 name possibilities. And I didn't do this, but my neighbors who did, who set up the process to try to whittle 32 name possibilities down to what it became La Burnham Park Boulevard was a fascinating little kind of you know, town meetings, democracy and action effort. The neighbors who did that really deserve a lot of thanks because it wasn't easy. But we found a path forward to making a change. And I came to see that merely addition isn't enough. Sometimes it needs to be about subtraction because you can't change history. You can't undo it. You can't rewrite it. You can't whitewash it. But sometimes you can choose who to honor. And a statue isn't about telling history, a statue is about honoring someone. And people who we honored, you know, 100 years ago as we look at it now, they're not worthy of being honored.",
