    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97115.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 87,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it really, I think we all need a little bit of therapy, like nature therapy, where you're attuned to bird sounds and rushing water or the sound of leaves falling through other leaves in the fall. We are so focused on screens. I mean, it's not unusual to walk into a Senate committee and every person, except the witness who is speaking, is looking at a screen. And I'm guilty of that. I'm guilty of that. And so the time in nature to kind of try to unplug was extremely helpful from a mental health standpoint. And remember, I'm doing this during COVID and impeachment trials. And I have long COVID that I'm dealing with. It's a very tumultuous time. I think I say in the book that I never could have done this until this point in my life when I'm 65. I never could have done it before. I couldn't have done it when I had kids at home. I couldn't have done it at many points along the way. But I never needed it as much as I needed it right now because what was going on in society was so challenging. What was going on in my work was so challenging. And so I really learned the value of time and nature. There's a Senate gym that's one floor beneath my office in the Senate. I don't think I've been in it once since I came back from that hike and trip in 2019. I do all my exercise outdoors now. If the weather's bad, okay, like I don't know somebody said that there's no such thing as bad weather, only the wrong clothes. You just wear different clothes if it's snowing or if it's raining. And that way you get not only the exercise, but you get the kind of mental health boost of being outdoors and paying attention to the wider world. The trip also really helped me on my 27-hour surgery on Interstate 95. I'd had to learn how to go hours with no water. hours with no food. And so that trip, I'm trying to get up here for a voting rights meeting. It's in January of 2022. And, you know, it's gonna be a snowstorm, but I grew up in the Midwest. I don't know how to drive in snow. I get on 995 in Richmond. The sign says snow, proceed with caution. It doesn't say turn around, stop, danger. I know how to proceed with caution. It turns into a 27-hour trip. I have no food or water in the car. stuck in the middle of the night, temperature 18 degrees on 995. A family gives me an orange and I end up finding a Dr. Pepper when I'm finding a place to get some gas. But what I learned was I was a cranky commuter for the first four or five hours of this. I got to get to DC for this meeting. Then I realized this in the commute, this is a survival test. I have a third of a tank of gas. It's 18 degrees. We've been stuck for hours, I have no idea when we're gonna get unstuck. Okay, how do I survive? When my mind switched from commute to survive, I switched from cranky to, okay, here's a problem I gotta solve. And then all this, the nature time and the survival skills from the trip kind of snapped into place and I figured out how to do it.",
