    "turn_text": "I'm going to so that you're right. The names are in the book, but I didn't do an index. I'm too lazy to do it, I guess. Some cool places. It's a pain. Yeah, it is a pain to do an index, right? So cool places on the trail. I'll go each segment. Mountain Home Cabin in Front Royal, Great Place, Great B&B that's right on the trail as it crosses Route 522. Ten site A20 in Loft Mountain Campground on the Skyline Drive. Amazing. That's a great campground all around. But that particular tent site is renowned. And I guess I could have kept it quiet and kept it as a secret. But I not only listed the number but even put a picture of it in the book and it's the view a second to none. That the Mountain Lake Hotel in a little bit west of Roanoke near Newport, Virginia, great place to stay. the Appalachian Trail kind of runs through the property, maybe a mile or so from the hotel itself. That's a great one. Woods Hole Hostel, a quirky log cabin hostel about 400 yards off the trail south of Paris, Virginia. Great place to stay. And then the shelters. Sometimes I'd stay You know what a B&B, oftentimes I would stay in this bivvy, this tiny little half-tent that I have that only weighs a pound. I'd stay there a lot, but I'd stay in shelters along the trail. And my favorite was the Chestnut Knob Shelter, which is a shelter that is a converted fire wardens cabin. at the southern end of Berks Garden, which is a truly remarkable valley in Tazwall County in southwest Virginia. So that was one I love. On the bike trip, we stayed on places, you know, the classic places along the Blurge Parkway and Skyline Drive, peaks of Otter, big meadows, We went and stayed at Hotel Floyd and heard a bunch of music. So those are some great ones. And then on the canoe trip, a lot of camping, two great state parks, James River State Park and Powhatan State Park camped in both of those places, stayed at a remarkable B&B in Charles City County where I had an incredible interaction with the owner, Nanny was her name and the B&B is North Bend Plantation and we had a really interesting discussion about her family history and the history of that particular land that had been settled and on which a number of slave and slave people worked back in the first part of the 1800s. And her story about the land and about what happened there was one of the most captivating chance encounters I had with anyone on the trip.",
