    "turn_text": "there's a nature deficit disorder. I can't remember the writer who claimed that phrase, but it just kind of captures a reality where people are transfixed by screens and spending less time outdoors. If you dig into some of the evidence on it, maybe the evidence doesn't completely support the proposition, particularly since COVID. I think COVID pushed a lot of people outdoors and they found I like being outdoors. you know, outdoor outfitters and bike shops and have done generally pretty good business sense. But I think we just really have to be intentional about this. And we have to be intentional about it for a couple of reasons. People who spend time outdoors, it's much better for your health and your mental health. But also, our outdoors needs stewards and protectors. And if you don't get into the habit of spending time outdoors, you're probably not going to be a steward or protector of the outdoors. And the best stewards and protectors are people who get into it. I mean, I give in the book, my parents, a Boy Scout troop at Curie of Arts Catholic Church, troop 395, and then a middle school shop teacher, Joe McKenzie. Those are the people who early in life kind of got me out into nature and to camping and canoeing. And thank God they did. I mean, they opened up a part of my life that might not have been open otherwise that's given me such tremendous satisfaction. Not every young person has that in their life. And so creating more opportunities where we can get young people out for their own sake, but also so that we know that we'll have the stewards of our outdoor resources forever. That's really important.",
