    "turn_idx": 103,
    "turn_text": "What did I learn? In that section, right at the end of the epilogue, I'm trying to sum up, what has life in the country been during the time I've done this? What's my Rorschach word? And the word humbling comes to mind. And the root word of humbling is grounded. To be in politics, humbling in humility is not the word we use. We tend to be self-important and puffed up, and we want to talk about American exceptionalism, America's the indispensable nation. That's kind of the way we do things in politics. And yet, if you think from a spiritual standpoint, and I use the phrase from the New Testament, he will humbles himself, she'll be exalted, he will exalt himself, she'll be humbled. If the character of humility is an important thing for an individual, Is it unimportant for a society? No, I think it should be important for a society. So what does it mean for a society to be humble? And so I grapple with that a little bit. And then I ask the question, OK, that's the word I would think about is life in the country during the last two years with COVID, with the attacks on the Capitol, with the impeachment trial. with racial justice protest, humbling is what came to mind. And then I said, what do I say about myself in this journey? And the Rorschach word that came to mind was grounding, which is that root word for humbling. You know, having been in politics, it was 25 years when I started. Now it's 30 years. You know, getting a grounding experience, it's not all about what bill I'm introduced, and it's not all about the roundtable or the interview with the local media. It's not even all just about communications with people. Virginia is gorgeous. It's my adopted state. I moved here 40 years ago. The natural bounty of Virginia, and everybody's got their favorite place. And that's a unifier in a divided time wanting to protect these beautiful places so that our kids and grandkids can drive the same joy from them that we do, that's a unifier. And I think the grounding experience, I gained some knowledge about our country and I had some epiphanies about why are certain things going on. I have a long analysis of the book of Job as I'm trying to think about what explains this moment in life and some of the challenges our country is going through. I think about the book of Job, which offers some incredible wisdom for a book that's 2,500 years old. It shows a very astute understanding of human psychology. Again, it's a story about a person, an allegory about a person, but you can expand its wisdom to thinking about societies as well. You need time to let these thoughts bubble up in your head. You know, if you're just running around doing your schedule every day, sometimes the thoughts don't bubble up or they don't crystallize. And one of the things about the journey is that thoughts that were kind of inchoate or rattling around in my head could kind of connect together and give me a greater understanding about the value of humility, about how to understand the challenges that America and the world are going through right now. the",
